    "final_answer": "Based on my navigation of the BBC News website, here is what I found in the Artificial Intelligence section:\n\n## Top Headline\n**\"Veteran conned by AI ex-soldier's 'pity scam'\"**\n\nThe story, published 2 hours ago by Jonathan Holmes (West of England), reports that:\n- Experts have warned of a growing wave of AI-generated \"pity scams\" using fake videos of injured ex-military personnel to sell products online.\n- Army veteran Raymond Gilbert, 63, from Bradenstoke, Wiltshire, was scammed by an account claiming to belong to a fellow ex-serviceman.\n- He spent £154.99 on a \"handmade\" wooden flag that turned out to be made in China.\n- The BBC identified 45 similar TikTok accounts, plus more on other platforms, selling military-themed items.\n\n## Companies Involved\n- **TikTok** — the primary platform named in the article. A TikTok spokesperson was quoted, stating the company has \"strict rules against harmfully misleading AI-generated content and scams\" and confirmed it had \"removed the content and accounts shared with them.\"\n\nThe story also alludes to other unnamed platforms and a Chinese manufacturer of the product, but **TikTok** is the only specific company explicitly named and engaged in the article.",
